<!--
  A page that THROWS while rendering — the error-boundary probe.

  Every other throwing shape in this fixture has been fixed at its cause (the
  `$extends` base import, the setup-body DOM access). This one is deliberately
  unfixable: it is a stand-in for the open-ended class of runtime failures a
  real app's route can hit — a loader whose D1 query rejects, a KV binding that
  is not wired, a template that reads a property off `undefined`.

  Before the boundary existed, a throw anywhere on that chain rejected the
  Worker's `fetch` promise, which on Cloudflare is error 1101 with NO HTTP
  response body at all — not a 500. The assertion is therefore that a response
  comes back, and that it is a text/plain 500 rather than a document.

  `@state` rather than a `loader` export on purpose: the compiler emits an
  `@state` block verbatim into the setup body that BOTH server renderers run,
  so this throws from inside `renderToString` — the deepest point on the chain
  and the one furthest from the handler that has to catch it.
-->

@route {
  name: "probe-boom"
  layout: "app"
}

@state {
  throw new Error('E2E-BOOM: this page throws while rendering')
}

@template {
  <main>
    <h1>BOOM-SHOULD-NOT-RENDER</h1>
  </main>
}
